    "editorialStatus": "draft",
    "image": "/images/title-covers/day1-roas-vs-roi-20261103.webp",
    "alt": "Marketer comparing attributed advertising revenue with a separate product-cost and campaign-profit ledger.",
    "imageCaption": "Topic illustration. All financial examples in the article are fictional.",
    "html": "<p><strong>ROAS divides attributed conversion revenue by ad spend. ROI compares a defined net profit with a defined investment cost. A high ROAS can coexist with a loss when product, fulfillment, refunds and campaign costs absorb the revenue.</strong></p><p>Every amount in the worked case is an original fictional USD planning assumption. It is not an industry benchmark, a customer result or a prediction.</p><h2 id=\"formula\">Write both formulas and state what each includes</h2><p><strong>ROAS = attributed conversion revenue ÷ advertising spend.</strong> A 4× result means $4 attributed revenue for each $1 of media spend; it is not $4 profit. Where a platform tracks non-revenue conversion value, call the metric conversion-value ROAS rather than cash sales.</p><p><strong>ROI = net return ÷ defined investment cost × 100%.</strong> Net return subtracts the costs included in that definition. Some teams report campaign profit divided by media/marketing investment; others divide by all included costs. Both can be interpretable, but only if the numerator and denominator are explicit and comparable.</p><h2 id=\"ledger\">Work through one completed campaign</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Original fictional campaign, one matched reporting window</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Ledger item</th><th scope=\"col\">Amount</th><th scope=\"col\">Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Net attributed revenue</th><td>$12,000</td><td>After credits/returns in this example</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Ad spend</th><td>$3,000</td><td>Media only</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Product costs</th><td>$5,400</td><td>Cost of fulfilled sales</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Fulfillment and transaction fees</th><td>$1,800</td><td>Additional sale-linked costs</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Creative and campaign support</th><td>$1,200</td><td>Separate from media</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Included total cost</th><td>$11,400</td><td>Sum of all four cost lines</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Profit after included costs</th><td>$600</td><td>$12,000 − $11,400</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>ROAS is $12,000 ÷ $3,000 = <strong>4×, or 400%</strong>. Total-cost ROI is $600 ÷ $11,400 = <strong>5.26%</strong>. A separately labelled marketing-investment return is $600 ÷ ($3,000 + $1,200) = <strong>14.29%</strong>. The different percentages do not conflict: their cost bases differ. The case excludes general company overhead, interest and tax, so $600 is not represented as company net income.</p><h2 id=\"threshold\">Find the threshold implied by this exact ledger</h2><p>Product and fulfillment costs total $7,200, or 60% of net revenue. The pre-marketing contribution ratio is 40%. Ignoring the separate creative/support cost, media-only break-even ROAS is 1 ÷ 0.40 = 2.5×. Including $1,200 creative/support and holding $3,000 media spend fixed requires revenue of ($3,000 + $1,200) ÷ 0.40 = $10,500, or <strong>3.5× media ROAS</strong>.</p><p>This threshold assumes a stable cost ratio, net revenue definition and campaign support budget. If returns, order mix or fulfillment rates change, recalculate. Use the existing <a href=\"/calculators/break-even-roas-calculator/\">break-even ROAS calculator</a> and its <a href=\"/blogs/break-even-roas-formula/\">formula explanation</a> for the threshold task rather than creating a second ROAS tool.</p><h2 id=\"sensitivity\">Keep the sales result constant and change the economics</h2><p>If fulfillment and fees increase by $900, profit falls from $600 to <strong>−$300</strong> while ROAS remains 4×. Included costs become $12,300 and total-cost ROI becomes −2.44%. That is why creative changes that raise revenue can still damage profit when they also increase discounts, returns or expensive deliveries.</p><p>Track net revenue, contribution before media, campaign profit and cash settlement together. Compare cohorts only after a consistent conversion/return window. An order reported today may be returned later, and a payment processor can settle cash on another date.</p><h2 id=\"attribution\">Do not turn attribution into proof of incremental sales</h2><p>Attributed conversions follow a platform’s measurement model. They do not establish how many sales would have happened without advertising. Separate branded/returning-customer attribution from a documented incrementality test. Do not combine overlapping platform revenue totals as though every order were unique.</p><p>Before increasing spend, verify currency, revenue events, refunds, attribution windows, cost imports and the investment definition. Review <a href=\"/blogs/how-to-price-products-for-ecommerce/\">ecommerce unit economics</a> and the <a href=\"/calculators/business-roi-calculator/\">business ROI calculator</a> when the decision includes more than advertising.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Use the worksheet with your own records</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/roas-vs-roi-worksheet.html\" download>Download the printable, editable HTML worksheet</a>. It contains the completed example and a blank table. Open it in a browser, edit the blank fields, calculate the outputs using the stated method and print to PDF or paper. It does not automatically calculate results or replace your accounting records.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Method references and source notes</h2><p>The method references below support the definitions. The examples, assumptions and decision checks on this page are independently constructed.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/14090?hl=en\">Google Ads: return on investment and cost boundaries</a></li><li><a href=\"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/14102450?hl=en\">Google Ads: attributed ROAS versus incremental ROAS</a></li></ul>",
