    "alt": "Business owner comparing an equipment investment with future dated cash-flow cards and a capital-project folder.",
    "imageCaption": "Topic illustration. All financial examples in the article are fictional.",
    "html": "<p><strong>Profitability index compares the present value of a project’s future net cash flows with its positive initial investment. Under the stated conventional setup, PI above 1 corresponds to positive NPV, but the ratio alone does not select the best combination of projects.</strong></p><p>Every amount in the worked case is an original fictional USD planning assumption. It is not an industry benchmark, a customer result or a prediction.</p><h2 id=\"formula\">State the cash-flow convention before calculating PI</h2><p><strong>PI = present value of future net project cash flows ÷ initial investment.</strong> Here the investment occurs at time zero, future amounts are net incremental project cash flows, and discounting uses end-of-year timing. <strong>NPV = present value of future net cash flows − initial investment.</strong> With these conventions and a positive initial outlay, PI = 1 + NPV ÷ initial investment.</p><p>Some sources use a different numerator or treat later investment outlays separately. Do not compare such ratios without reconciling the definitions. A zero initial investment makes this version undefined. Nonconventional cash flows and multiple later capital commitments need an explicitly rebuilt model.</p><h2 id=\"case\">Discount the same project year by year</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Original fictional project; 10% illustrative discount rate</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Time</th><th scope=\"col\">Net project cash flow</th><th scope=\"col\">Discount calculation</th><th scope=\"col\">Present value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">0</th><td>−$20,000</td><td>Initial investment</td><td>−$20,000.00</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Year 1</th><td>$9,000</td><td>9000 ÷ 1.10</td><td>$8,181.82</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Year 2</th><td>$9,000</td><td>9000 ÷ 1.10²</td><td>$7,438.02</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Year 3</th><td>$9,000</td><td>9000 ÷ 1.10³</td><td>$6,761.83</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Future PV total</th><td>$27,000 nominal</td><td>Sum of unrounded PV</td><td>$22,381.67</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>PI is $22,381.6679 ÷ $20,000 = <strong>1.1191</strong>. NPV is <strong>$2,381.67</strong>. Retain unrounded discount factors until the final display. The 10% rate is an illustrative input, not a recommended return, borrowing quote or risk-adjusted rate for your business.</p><h2 id=\"meaning\">A ratio above one is not an annual return</h2><p>A PI of 1.1191 means about $1.1191 of discounted future net cash flows for each $1 initially invested in this defined case. It is not an 11.91% annual ROI. Project timing, risk, taxes, replacement needs and a suitable discount rate affect the interpretation. Accounting profit and EBITDA are not substitutes for incremental project cash flow.</p><p>At the same 10% discount rate, reducing each future annual cash flow to $8,000 gives future PV of about $19,894.82, PI of 0.9947 and NPV of −$105.18. A modest assumption change crosses the acceptability threshold, so retain a sensitivity table rather than only the base-case label.</p><h2 id=\"scale\">Use NPV and constraints when comparing alternatives</h2><p>Consider project A requiring $10,000 with future PV $13,000: PI is 1.30 and NPV $3,000. Project B requires $30,000 with future PV $36,000: PI is 1.20 and NPV $6,000. If the projects are mutually exclusive and risk, timing and other constraints are comparable, choosing the higher ratio alone would miss B’s larger dollar NPV.</p><p>Under a fixed budget with indivisible projects, evaluate feasible combinations. Dividing a project into fractions is not automatically possible. Include dependencies, available funding, staff capacity, required assets and strategic obligations. A ratio ranking can assist analysis; it is not a universal capital-allocation rule.</p><h2 id=\"inputs\">Build a defensible project cash schedule</h2><ol><li>Specify the decision and the alternative being compared.</li><li>List incremental cash inflows, operating outflows, working-capital changes, later investment and terminal recoveries.</li><li>Use consistent timing, currency, nominal/real treatment and tax assumptions.</li><li>Document the chosen discount rate and review sensitivities.</li><li>Reconcile PI to NPV and confirm feasibility before approval.</li></ol><p>Use the <a href=\"/blogs/present-value-vs-future-value/\">present-value guide</a> for discounting, the <a href=\"/calculators/business-roi-calculator/\">business investment calculator</a> for its stated return/payback measures, and the <a href=\"/calculators/cash-flow-forecast-template/\">cash forecast</a> to check funding dates. A discounted value result does not ensure cash is available to buy the asset.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Use the worksheet with your own records</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/profitability-index-worksheet.html\" download>Download the printable, editable HTML worksheet</a>. It contains the completed example and a blank table. Open it in a browser, edit the blank fields, calculate the outputs using the stated method and print to PDF or paper. It does not automatically calculate results or replace your accounting records.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Method references and source notes</h2><p>The method references below support the definitions. The examples, assumptions and decision checks on this page are independently constructed.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://corporatefinanceinstitute.com/resources/accounting/profitability-index/\">CFI: profitability-index formula and interpretation</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.wallstreetprep.com/knowledge/profitability-index/\">Wall Street Prep: PI calculation and comparison limits</a></li></ul>",
